<!--
  /motion — the page must MOVE. An argument about animation made in prose is
  self-refuting, and this page was exactly that.

  Everything below is live. The timeline section animates as you scroll it: `view`
  attaches a native ViewTimeline, which the browser drives on the compositor —
  no JS in the frame loop.

  Authored under the floor: no <style>, no raw <div>, no inline style.
-->
<column class="doc" gap="5">
  <p class="eyebrow">What's given</p>
  <h1>Motion</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    One directive. <code>motion:</code> takes a <strong>trigger</strong> — when —
    and a <strong>config</strong> — what moves. There is no second animation API,
    no transition directive, and no imperative escape hatch you are meant to
    graduate to. Everything on this page is running right now.
  </p>

  <h2>Interaction</h2>
  <p>
    The browser already knows when these happen, so the only question left is what
    they should look like. Hover the cards. Press them.
  </p>

  <grid cols="3" gap="3">
    <card motion:hover={{ scale: 'spring:gentle' }} sx="p:4">
      <strong>hover</strong>
      <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">spring:gentle</p>
    </card>
    <card motion:hover={{ scale: 'spring:bouncy' }} sx="p:4">
      <strong>hover</strong>
      <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">spring:bouncy</p>
    </card>
    <card motion:press={{ scale: 'press' }} sx="p:4">
      <strong>press</strong>
      <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">press</p>
    </card>
  </grid>

  <code-block code={code.interaction}></code-block>

  <h2>Lifecycle</h2>
  <p>
    <code>enter</code> and <code>exit</code>. These are the ones a framework has
    to be involved in: <strong>exit holds the element in the DOM until its
    animation finishes.</strong> Remove a node yourself and its exit animation has
    nowhere to play.
  </p>

  <row gap="3" align="center">
    <button on:click={() => open.set(!open())}>
      {open() ? 'Remove' : 'Add'} the panel
    </button>
    <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">It finishes leaving before it goes.</p>
  </row>

  {#if open()}
    <card
      motion:enter={{ opacity: 'fade', scale: 'spring:snappy' }}
      motion:exit={{ opacity: 'fade-out' }}
      sx="p:5"
    >
      <strong>I animate in, and I animate out.</strong>
      <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">
        The exit is the part you cannot do by hand — the runtime keeps me mounted
        until I am done.
      </p>
    </card>
  {/if}

  <code-block code={code.lifecycle}></code-block>

  <h2>Stagger</h2>
  <p>
    <code>stagger</code> is reserved, not a property: it describes the
    relationship <em>between</em> siblings, not anything about any one of them.
    It is what makes a list arrive as a list instead of a flash.
  </p>

  <row gap="3" align="center">
    <button on:click={() => shown.set(!shown())}>
      {shown() ? 'Clear' : 'Deal'} the cards
    </button>
  </row>

  {#if shown()}
    <grid cols="4" gap="2">
      {#for n of items() key={n}}
        <card
          motion:enter={{ opacity: 'fade', translate-y: 'spring:snappy', stagger: '0.05s' }}
          sx="p:4"
        >
          <strong>{n}</strong>
        </card>
      {/for}
    </grid>
  {/if}

  <code-block code={code.stagger}></code-block>

  <h2>Timeline — scroll, not time</h2>
  <p>
    <code>view</code> and <code>scroll</code> are driven by scroll
    <em>position</em>, not by time. They attach a native
    <code>ViewTimeline</code>, which the browser drives on the compositor —
    <strong>no JavaScript runs while you scroll</strong>, only once at mount to
    set it up. The effect people install a 30&nbsp;kB library for is a directive
    with a preset.
  </p>
  <p sx="fg:muted-foreground">Keep scrolling. Each of these arrives on its own.</p>

  <column gap="4">
    <card motion:view={{ opacity: 'fade', translate-y: 'spring:gentle' }} sx="p:6">
      <h3>This one fades up as it enters the viewport.</h3>
      <p sx="fg:muted-foreground">
        <code>motion:view</code> — nothing in the frame loop.
      </p>
    </card>

    <card motion:view={{ opacity: 'fade', translate-x: 'spring:deliberate' }} sx="p:6">
      <h3>This one slides in, slowly.</h3>
      <p sx="fg:muted-foreground">
        The same directive, a different preset — <code>spring:deliberate</code>.
      </p>
    </card>

    <card motion:view={{ scale: 'spring:bouncy', opacity: 'fade' }} sx="p:6">
      <h3>And this one springs.</h3>
      <p sx="fg:muted-foreground">
        Nine presets. Four springs, five tweens. That is the whole catalogue.
      </p>
    </card>
  </column>

  <code-block code={code.timeline}></code-block>

  <h2>The vocabulary</h2>
  <p>
    Small on purpose, and enforced: an unknown trigger or preset is a compile
    error, not a silent no-op. A typo used to be something you could ship.
  </p>

  <grid cols="2" gap="4">
    <column gap="1">
      <label>Triggers</label>
      <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">hover · press · focus · focus-visible · enter · exit · view · scroll</p>
    </column>
    <column gap="1">
      <label>Properties</label>
      <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">scale · opacity · translate-x · translate-y · rotate · <em>stagger</em> (reserved)</p>
    </column>
    <column gap="1">
      <label>Springs</label>
      <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">spring:snappy · spring:gentle · spring:bouncy · spring:deliberate</p>
    </column>
    <column gap="1">
      <label>Tweens</label>
      <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">fade · fade-out · press · release · pulse</p>
    </column>
  </grid>

  <alert tone="info">
    <strong>Reduced motion is not your problem.</strong> Every
    <code>motion:</code> respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> — the
    JavaScript triggers check it, and the framework's global rule kills the CSS
    timelines. Honouring it is not something you can forget to do.
  </alert>

  <row class="page-nav" gap="2">
    <a class="btn" href="/theming">← Theming</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/behavior">Behavior →</a>
  </row>
</column>
